  "body": "There have been a few spoofs that use JS dialogs [[1](https://crbug.com/606104)][[2](https://crbug.com/634108)][[3](https://crbug.com/823353)], and in response some browsers have tried to identify the site triggering the alert in the dialog. This however leads to confusing UI in which users are shown a URL that is seen nowhere else, or an attempt at explaining the dialog is not from the top level page nor the browser (e.g. Chrome uses \"An embedded page on this page says:..\").\r\nBecause of this, in Chrome we are considering removing support for cross-origin iframes to trigger JS dialogs ([link to Intent to Remove](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/hTOXiBj3D6A/) discussion). \r\nThe spec allows early returning in all three cases (step 3 of [[4](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#dom-alert)][[5](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#dom-confirm)][[6](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#dom-prompt)]), but if other browsers are on board with a similar change, cross-origin iframes JS dialogs can be explicitly disallowed by adding an early return step before step 3 for the cross origin iframe case.\r\n\r\nChrome's use counter data says around 0.009% of page loads would be affected with this change (0.006% for [alert](https://www.chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity#CrossOriginWindowAlert), 0.003 for [confirm](https://www.chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity#CrossOriginWindowConfirm) and 0.00006 for [prompt](https://www.chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity#CrossOriginWindowPrompt)).",
